+*   **When to use:** Large-scale parallel simulations where data is too large to move for postprocessing, requiring in-situ or postprocess visualization [S2, unofficial].
+*   **When not to use:** Shared-memory architectures where simpler methods may be more efficient, or when dynamic load balancing is critical for high efficiency [S1, S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-This algorithm was proposed by researchers supported by NASA (NAS1-19480) while at ICASE, NASA Langley Research Center [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the challenges of visualizing large, unstructured-grid data from computational modeling (finite volume/element methods) on distributed-memory parallel computers like the Intel Paragon [S1, unofficial]. It differs from prior methods that used shared-memory multiprocessors (Williams [22], Uselton [19], Challinger [2]) or network-based master-slave approaches with data replication (Giertsen & Petersen [7]) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Origin:** Proposed by [S1, S2, unofficial] (author not explicitly named in the provided text, but associated with NASA ICASE).
+*   **Date:** 1996 (based on NTRS record date) [S2, unofficial].
+*   **History:** Developed to address the challenge of visualizing large, unstructured-grid data from finite volume/element methods on distributed-memory parallel computers like the Intel Paragon [S1, S2, unofficial]. It differs from prior methods by being completely distributed and less view-dependent [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Usage:** Designed primarily as a postprocess for large-scale parallel simulations, but flexible enough to support runtime monitoring of in-situ simulation data [S2, unofficial]. Prototype implementation handled only tetrahedral cells [S2, unofficial].
 
